  <running_head>CASTIGATIO PARAL.</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <l n="1" para="true">Nam licet intellectus, voluntas &amp; ap-</l>
    <l n="2">petitus permanserint, est tamen intelle-</l>
    <l n="3">ctus obtenebratus, voluntas deprava-</l>
    <l n="4">ta, &amp; inferior appetitus totaliter vitia-</l>
    <l n="5">tus, supernaturalia autem penitus sunt</l>
    <l n="6">sublata, nempe intellectûs claritas, vo-</l>
    <l n="7">luntatis rectitudo &amp; appetitus cum ra-</l>
    <l n="8">tione conformitas.</l>
    <l n="9" para="true">
      <i>Positiva</i> corruptio est, quâ præter il-</l>
    <l n="10">lam iustitiæ originalis carentiam homo</l>
    <l n="11">iam propensus est ad omne malum, id</l>
    <l n="12">est, ad odium Dei &amp; proximi. Scho-</l>
    <l n="13">lastici vocant inordinatam dispositio-</l>
    <l n="14">nem animæ. Thomas habitum corru-</l>
    <l n="15">ptum. Huius pravæ dispositionis re-</l>
    <l n="16">spectu Augustinus peccatum originale</l>
    <l n="17">vocat <i>vitium</i>, <i>quo claudicat omne huma-</i>
    </l>
    <l n="18">
      <i>num genus</i>; utriusque respectu appellat</l>
    <l n="19">
      <i>cœnum</i>, <i>quo omnes nascuntur squalidi</i>.</l>
    <l n="20">Sacra vero scriptura eandem, <i>concupi-</i>
    </l>
    <l n="21">
      <i>scentiam</i>, nominat. Jacob. 1: 14,15.</l>
    <l n="22" para="true">Concupiscentiæ (ἐπιθυμίας) vocabu-</l>
    <l n="23">lum ambiguum est, significat enim</l>
    <l n="24">physicè,</l>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Greek word in the penultimate paragraph is printed as ἐπιθυμίας (transcribed from the scan; the scan is slightly blurred). Page is a continuous text page with no marginal notes.</notes>
</page>
